{"name":"Stone Mason","hex":"#959897","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"},{"term":"mason","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bricklayer, one whose occupation is to build with stone or brick.","One who prepares stone for building purposes."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To build stonework or brickwork about, under, in, over, etc.; to construct by masons"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mason"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stonemasonry","blocks":[{"text":"Stonemasonry, also called stonework or stonecraft, is the creation of buildings, structures, and sculpture using stone as the primary material. Stonemasonry is the craft of shaping and arranging stones, often together with mortar and even the ancient lime mortar, to wall or cover formed structures."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Gobekli_Tepe_5.jpg/330px-Gobekli_Tepe_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonemasonry","wikidata":"Q19794820"},{"title":"Mason (surname)","blocks":[{"text":"Mason is an occupational surname of Scottish and English origin, with variations also found in Italian and French, historically referring to someone who performed stonemasonry work. The surname Mason was originally brought to England in the great wave of migration following the Norman Conquest of 1066. The name Mason is for a stone-mason. The name was originally derived from the Old English or Old French word masson."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mason_(surname)","wikidata":"Q12664711"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)","wikidata":"Q8063"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/2016_Angkor%2C_Banteay_Kdei_%2820%29.jpg/500px-2016_Angkor%2C_Banteay_Kdei_%2820%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/2016_Angkor%2C_Banteay_Kdei_%2820%29.jpg/1280px-2016_Angkor%2C_Banteay_Kdei_%2820%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/2016_Angkor%2C_Banteay_Kdei_%2820%29.jpg/500px-2016_Angkor%2C_Banteay_Kdei_%2820%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"12th-century stonemasonry at Angkor Wat","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2016_Angkor%2C_Banteay_Kdei_(20).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Diamond_Wire_for_marble_mining.JPG/500px-Diamond_Wire_for_marble_mining.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikime